    "body": "### Contact Details (e.g. Tox ID or email)\n\ntox:FE14302CB396D75AFD3DD7500D54B2A5A914EE7824052D13F1B1CAC67FBED47654B195652079\n\n### qTox Version\n\nv1.18.3\n\n### Commit Hash\n\nb7802b42dcf5f68474eb4d6db66c004e3ca50848\n\n### Toxcore Version\n\n0.2.20\n\n### Qt Version\n\n6.8.1\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Start qTox (using the first profile via an export file).\n2. Start qTox (using the second profile via an export file).\n2. Log in to both profiles.\n3. Start a call with a friend (using only one of the profiles).\n4. **The bug:** at some point, the call was suddenly ended without us noticing.\n5. Close the second profile (the one not being used for the call).\n6. No further problems occurred during the call.\n\n### Observed Behavior\n\n**Note:** this was not my intention; I have two qTox profiles and realised that both could be accessed simultaneously if you ran your export file from a specific profile. In fact, I only needed to leave a second profile connected.\n\nI realised that this could be a potential problem, (i believe) as qTox was not designed to be accessed by multiple profiles simultaneously on the same device (this is possible if you open the export file).\n\nI came to this conclusion because if you start qTox normally from your usual shortcut, it does not allow you to open another account, but only flashes on the taskbar indicating that it has already been opened.\n\n_I believed that the problem was the second logged-in profile, since when I logged it out, the problem ceased. In addition, we can run multiple profiles together._\n\n### Expected Behavior\n\nIt would be expected that when you run an export file with a profile already connected, qTox would not allow you to do the same when you try to reopen it using the main qTox shortcut (not an export file).\n\nWith an export file, you can log into multiple profiles simultaneously on the same PC.\n\n### Reproducibility\n\nAlways\n\n### Operating System\n\nWindows\n\n### Operating System version/distro/name/etc.\n\nWindows 11 Version 25H2\n\n### Relevant log output\n\n```shell\n\n```",
